# Hub Diagnostics

Connect to the Aqara hub through Telnet and inspect the process list:

```sh
ps
```

Check processes related to the audio port:

```sh
ps | grep 12346
```

Check the `aplay` process:

```sh
ps | grep aplay
```

The BusyBox version on the hub may not support:

```sh
grep -E
```

Use separate `grep` commands instead.

Example of suspended processes:

```text
348 admin 1584 T nc -l -p 12346
1940 admin 1584 T nc -l -p 12346
8679 admin 1584 T nc -l -p 12346
```

The `T` state means the process is stopped or suspended.

A normal active audio session may look like:

```text
1995 admin 1584 S nc -l -p 12346
1996 admin 2512 S aplay -t raw -f S32_LE -c 1 -r 32000
```

The `S` state is normal sleeping or waiting behavior.

---

# Manual Cleanup

Suspended processes can be terminated using their individual PIDs:

```sh
kill -9 348 1940 8679
```

Do not use:

```sh
killall nc
```

The hub also uses `nc` for MQTT-related services:

```text
nc -l -p 1884
nc 127.0.0.1 1883
```

Killing all `nc` processes can interrupt MQTT communication and break the integration.

---

# Automatic Cleanup Watchdog

Create the watchdog script:

```sh
cat > /data/scripts/audio_cleanup.sh <<'EOF'
#!/bin/sh

while true
do
    ps | awk '
        $4 ~ /^T/ &&
        $0 ~ /nc -l -p 12346/ {
            print $1
        }
    ' | while read PID
    do
        if [ -n "$PID" ]; then
            echo "$(date) Removing suspended audio process PID=$PID" >> /tmp/audio_cleanup.log
            kill -9 "$PID" 2>/dev/null
        fi
    done

    sleep 30
done
EOF
```

Make it executable:

```sh
chmod +x /data/scripts/audio_cleanup.sh
```

Start it manually:

```sh
/data/scripts/audio_cleanup.sh >/dev/null 2>&1 &
```

Verify that it is running:

```sh
ps | grep audio_cleanup
```

Read the cleanup log:

```sh
cat /tmp/audio_cleanup.log
```

---

# Automatic Startup After Reboot

If `/data/scripts/mqtt_tunnel.sh` already starts automatically during boot, the watchdog can be launched from that script:

```sh
cat >> /data/scripts/mqtt_tunnel.sh <<'EOF'

# Watchdog for suspended audio processes
if ! ps | grep audio_cleanup.sh | grep -v grep >/dev/null
then
    /data/scripts/audio_cleanup.sh >/dev/null 2>&1 &
fi
EOF
```

Before appending the block, verify that it has not already been added:

```sh
grep audio_cleanup /data/scripts/mqtt_tunnel.sh
```

